    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 041757\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1056 AM PDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above-normal temperatures continue through Friday, with dangerous \n  heat returning to Death Valley.\n\n* A trough is set to bring cooler temperatures, increased winds\n  and low precipitation chances next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Sunday.\n\nEnsemble guidance is in good agreement on anomalous ridging \nremaining in place through the upcoming work week. It looks like a \nweak disturbance will slide around the northern and eastern \nperiphery of the area on Monday-Tuesday, but this won't do much \nother then briefly knock temperatures down a few degrees. Highs are \nforecast to be 8-12 degrees above normal, with lows 5-10 degrees \nabove normal. This will be most pronounced in Death Valley where \nhighs are expected to be near 110 and overnight lows only getting \ndown into the mid 80s. Because of the resultant major to extreme \nheat risk, an Extreme Heat Warning has been issue for elevations \nbelow 2000 feet in Death Valley from 11am this morning through 8pm \nFriday.\n\nBy next weekend, we should begin to feel the effects of our next \nsystem. Still a ways out so uncertainty remains, but ensembles \nsuggest we could see a notable cooldown accompanied by gusty \nsouthwest winds. Precipitation potential will hinge on the \ninteraction with Hurricane Rachel in the east Pacific. Latest NBM \nhas low PoPs (15%) in the Sierra as early as Saturday, with a broad \nsmattering of 5-25% across the area on Sunday. These are likely to \nchange over the coming days as we get more clarity on the trough-\nhurricane interaction, but certainly something to monitor.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds following typical daily directional patterns will persist in \nthe terminal area through Monday. The chance of seeing sustained \nspeeds of 10 knots or greater continues to be less than 10 to 20 \npercent, although a random gust to 15 knots cannot be ruled out \nduring peak afternoon heating today and again on Monday. VFR \nconditions will prevail through the TAF period, with only a few high \nclouds with bases AOA 20kft AGL expected during the afternoons.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light winds following \ntypical daily directional patterns are expected at most regional TAF \nsites through Monday. Two exceptions will be the northern Owens \nValley, including KBIH, where southerly winds gusting as high as 25 \nknots are expected this afternoon, and the western Mojave Desert, \nincluding KDAG, where easterly wind gusts to around 30 knots are \npossible this afternoon and early evening. VFR conditions will \nprevail through Monday in all areas, with only a few high clouds \nwith bases AOA 20kft AGL expected. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
